{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_fhororyigcrvzhydjutl35magdr3puwt4hragniiix7dkjdm6dgq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary of work done:\n\n## Phase 1: Translation\nImplemented `src/morton.rs`:\n- `morton(hi, lo) -> u64` — interleaves bits of `hi` (into odd positions) and `lo` (into even positions), translated from C's bit-spreading sequence.\n- `unmortoner(x) -> u32` — extracts every other bit (the even-indexed bits) and packs them into a `u32`.\n- `unmorton(z) -> Morton` — produces a `Morton` struct using `unmortoner(z)` for `hi` and `unmortoner(z >> 1)` for `lo`, matching the C semantics exactly.\n\nAll implementations are pure safe Rust (no `unsafe`, no FFI).\n\n## Phase 2: Tests\nCompiled the C sources to obtain ground-truth values for assertions, then created two test files in `src/bin/`:\n\n- **`src/bin/test_morton.rs`** — 15 tests for `morton()` covering zero, basic small values, the `0b1100 / 0b0011` example from the C documentation comment, all-ones, the literal C-test assertion `morton(0x347210d1, 0xc6843fad) == 0x5a346a180755e653`, hi-only, lo-only, MSB-only, alternating bit patterns, decimal, and a misc 32-bit pattern.\n\n- **`src/bin/test_unmorton.rs`** — 23 tests covering both `unmorton()` and `unmortoner()`: zero, single bit cases, `0xFF`, `0x5555…`, `0xAAAA…`, all-ones, the inverse of the C-test known value, a misc 64-bit pattern, plus a roundtrip test that mirrors the C test loop's assertions exactly (including the deliberate hi/lo swap in `unmorton`'s output).\n\n`cargo build` is clean and `cargo test` reports **38 passed; 0 failed**."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":267,"cache_read_input_tokens":41068,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":267,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"sess